WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.280
A stroke deep in the left side of the brain weakens the right side of the body.

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.220
Why the opposite side?

3
00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:13.933
A cut through the right half of the cord in the neck weakens the right side too.

4
00:00:14.893 --> 00:00:17.833
Lesions on opposite sides, weakness on the same side.

5
00:00:18.417 --> 00:00:22.917
Follow the tract down from the motor cortex, through the internal capsule and the

6
00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:23.917
brainstem.

7
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.450
In the caudal medulla, most of its fibres cross the midline.

8
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.670
That crossing is the pyramidal decussation.

9
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:35.053
Damage above the crossing shows up on the opposite side of the body.

10
00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:38.107
In the cord, most fibres have already crossed.

11
00:00:38.907 --> 00:00:41.007
So damage there shows up on the same side.

12
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.030
So here is the first count.

13
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.150
Count the crossings between the damage and the muscle.

14
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:48.410
One crossing flips the side.

15
00:00:48.983 --> 00:00:53.883
Try one. A lesion sits in the right pyramid, just above the crossing.

16
00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:55.883
Which limbs are weak?

17
00:01:01.333 --> 00:01:06.113
The left limbs. One crossing lies below the lesion, so the side flips.

18
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:12.567
A second puzzle. The basal ganglia never reach the spinal cord, yet in the classic

19
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.267
model they decide whether a movement goes ahead.

20
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.460
Their trick rests on inhibition.

21
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:19.960
Picture three neurons in a row.

22
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.200
The first inhibits the second, and the second inhibits the third.

23
00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.250
Now the first neuron starts firing faster.

24
00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:29.330
What does the third one do?

25
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:36.590
The third fires faster too.

26
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.970
The first holds the second down, so the second lets go of the third.

27
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:44.393
Inhibiting an inhibitor releases its target.

28
00:01:45.213 --> 00:01:46.253
That is disinhibition.

29
00:01:46.933 --> 00:01:48.313
That is the second count.

30
00:01:49.213 --> 00:01:51.013
Each inhibitory link flips the sign.

31
00:01:51.893 --> 00:01:53.473
An even number keeps the direction.

32
00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:55.853
An odd number reverses it.

33
00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:57.546
Put them together.

34
00:01:58.386 --> 00:02:02.386
For a motor pathway, follow the chain from the lesion or drug to the muscle.

35
00:02:03.446 --> 00:02:04.446
Crossings set the side.

36
00:02:05.366 --> 00:02:06.426
Inhibitions set the sign.

37
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.640
Some limits. The counts give the direction of a change along one chosen path, not

38
00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.040
its size. The sign count assumes each link keeps a fixed sign, and each neuron can

39
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:19.200
still change its firing.

40
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.280
When routes pull in opposite directions, the net effect depends on how strong each

41
00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:29.960
route is. And input from both sides breaks the crossing count: after a one-sided

42
00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.020
cortical lesion, the forehead is usually weakened less than the lower face.

43
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.590
Follow the chain, and count what flips.
